# Localized Electrosynthesis and Subsequent Electrochemical Mapping of Catalytically Active Metal–Organic Frameworks

## abstract
In recent years, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have shown great potential to be used as porous, high surface area catalytic materials capable of driving electrochemical energy conversion reactions. However, further improvement in their electrocatalytic performance necessitates methods to couple high‐throughput MOF synthesis and their subsequent electrochemical activity characterization. In this work, scanning electrochemical microscopy (SECM) is employed to perform a localized, micron‐scale electrosynthesis of two types of MOFs, Al2(OH)2‐TCPP, and HKUST‐1. SECM is also utilized to analyze the electrocatalytic hydrogen evolution reaction activity of the as‐prepared MOF micropatterns, via i) substrate‐generation tip‐collection mode to map the MOF's electrochemical reactivity, and ii) redox competition mode, to accurately extract the MOF's catalytic onset potential. Thus, the presented method provides a means to shed light on the operation principles of electroactive MOFs, toward their future incorporation in alternative fuel‐production schemes.

In recent years, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have shown great potential to be used as porous, high surface area catalytic materials capable of driving electrochemical energy conversion reactions. However, further improvement in their electrocatalytic performance necessitates methods to couple highthroughput MOF synthesis and their subsequent electrochemical activity characterization. In this work, scanning electrochemical microscopy (SECM) is employed to perform a localized, micron-scale electrosynthesis of two types of MOFs, $A l_{2}(O H)_{2}$ -TCPP, and HKUST-1. SECM is also utilized to analyze the electrocatalytic hydrogen evolution reaction activity of the as-prepared MOF micropatterns, via $\mathfrak{i}$ substrate-generation tip-collection mode to map the MOF’s electrochemical reactivity, and ii) redox competition mode, to accurately extract the MOF’s catalytic onset potential. Thus, the presented method provides a means to shed light on the operation principles of electroactive MOFs, toward their future incorporation in alternative fuel-production schemes.
## 1. Introduction
While global energy demands are constantly growing, we face a depleted reserve of fossil fuel and high carbon emissions associated with fuel combustion. Consequently, there is an urgent need to develop alternative sustainable and green energy resources. In this context, the electrocatalytically driven production of alternative fuels and chemicals is a valuable means to store renewable energy in the form of chemical bonds. As a result, in recent years, there has been a significant increase in research efforts aiming to develop highly efficient electrocatalysts to drive energy-conversion reactions as hydrogen evolution reaction (HER),[1] oxygen evolution reaction (OER),[2], and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.[3]
In that respect, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are an emerging class of materials with immense potential in electrochemical catalysis.[4] MOFs comprise periodic coordination networks containing multitopic organic linkers and metal-ion-containing nodes. These crystalline, highly porous solids have ultra-high surface areas,[5] and thus, they have traditionally attracted great interest for gas capture,[6] separation,[7], and storage[8] applications. Additionally, the modular nature of the MOF structures, which stems from the tunability of the linker and metal nodes, has also sparked interest in applications involving chemical catalysis,[9] and sensing.[10] An emerging field of research focuses on the incorporation of MOF thin films[11] into electrochemical[12] and solar energy conversion[13] systems. In recent years, MOFs have also shown great potential to be utilized in electrocatalytic energy-related schemes.[14] Generally, electrocatalytic MOFs possess several key advantages over conventional, dense heterogeneous solids.[15] They can potentially be used as a porous platform for the assembly of the extremely high density of catalytically active sites (molecular catalysts) while providing mass-transport conduits accessible for the diffusion of ions and catalytic substrates toward the active sites.[16]
We and several other groups have demonstrated the feasibility of utilizing MOF-based systems as a high surface area platform for driving electrocatalytic energy-conversion reactions such as HER,[17] $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction,[15a,18] oxygen reduction reaction (ORR),[16a,c,19] and OER.[14,20] Yet, in order to discover new electroactive MOFs and widen our understanding of electrocatalytic MOF-based systems, one needs to develop new means for their high-throughput synthesis and electrocatalytic activity screening.
In that respect, scanning electrochemical microscopy (SECM) is a powerful tool for fabricating patterned arrays of catalytic materials and their rapid in-situ evaluation toward electrocatalytic energy-related reactions.[21] Generally, an SECM setup is composed of a four-electrode cell configuration, with two working electrodes: i) an SECM ultramicroelectrode (UME) tip, and ii) a substrate electrode containing the catalytic material of interest (alongside a counter and a reference electrode).[22] A typical SECM operation principle is the substrate-generation tip-collection (SG/TC) mode. A catalytic product electrochemically formed at the substrate’s surface can be simultaneously detected and quantified by the tip electrode, which is placed in close proximity to the substrate (within a few micrometers).[22] Using this principle, SECM has been utilized for the analysis of various electrochemical experiments as imaging of corrosion processes,[22] detection of short-lived radical-intermediates during catalysis,[23] quantification of catalyst’s active-site density,[24] and rapid investigation of catalytic materials electrochemical activity.[21b] In addition, SECM could be used as a fabrication tool for local electrodeposition of a range of materials, e.g., metals,[25] polymers,[26] and semiconductors.[27] Generally, there are two approaches to perform confined electrodeposition. First, by oxidizing a metal-based tip (acting as a metal source) to release metal ions to the solution.[25] In turn, the metal ions are reduced back at the substrate electrode to form a localized metal deposit. Second, initializing deposition from solutionresiding precursors, either by generating active radicals at the tip to start a polymerization reaction at the substrate, or by electrochemically induced local pH change to initiate crystallization/precipitation at the surface.[26,28] Spatial confinement of the deposits is achieved by taking advantage of the unique conditions formed at the substrate–tip gap. Namely, maintaining a high concentration of reactive precursors near the substrate surface while impeding their diffusion outside the gap.
In recent years, there is an increased interest in the development of new ways to construct MOF-based thin films and explore their applications. Electrochemical deposition of MOFs is considered one of the promising methods for fabricating MOF films due to several reasons as i) mild synthesis ­conditions, ii) possibility for precise manipulation of reaction conditions, and iii) the potential for scaling-up the deposition process.[29] MOFs electrosynthesis can be done over a variety of conducting surfaces via either a cathodic or anodic approach. Anodic electrosynthesis is achieved by slow anodic dissolution of a metal electrode to release metal ions to a solution containing the desired MOF ligand, thus resulting in MOF formation over the metal surface.[30] On the contrary, in the cathodic deposition, both the metal source and the MOF ligand are dissolved in the electrolyte solution. A cathodic potential is applied over a conductive electrode to produce base equivalents, which in turn, deprotonate the MOF ligands to form the MOF over the electrode surface.[29,31]
Lately, by coupling SECM’s analytical and synthetical capabilities, we have developed a method for the localized electrochemical conversion of pristine MOFs into electroactive metal sulfides (e.g., $\mathrm{CoS_{x}}$ and $\mathrm{FeNiS_{x})}$ ) and subsequently study their electrocatalytic activity.[21a] Hence, we reasoned that an SECM-induced localized electrodeposition approach could be used to generate micropatterns of pristine MOFs, toward their in situ electrochemical performance investigation. Herein, as a proof of concept, we demonstrate the possibility to create localized patterns of two different MOFs: $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}.$ TCPP, and the well-known Cu-based MOF, HKUST-1. In addition, we have also assessed the capability of the SECM to evaluate the electrocatalytic activity of the as-formed MOF pattern. Being a porphyrin-based MOF, $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ -TCPP is electrochemically active, thus we have been able to image the MOF’s micropattern activity toward electrocatalytic HER. Additionally, using the SECM redox competition (RC) mode, we have been able to accurately determine the MOF’s catalytic onset potential. As such, the presented method could be expanded to perform rapid fabrication and analysis of electroactive MOFs toward their utilization on a wide range of catalytic reactions.
## 2. Results and Discussion
For the electrosynthesis of $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ -TCPP MOF micropatterns, first a thin alumina $(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3})$ layer (thickness of $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) was deposited on an FTO electrode, via electron-beam physical vapor deposition (termed ${\mathrm{FTO}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ ) (for more details, see Supporting Information Experimental Section and SEM images in Figure S1 Supporting Information). The FTO– ${\bf\cdot A l}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ substrate electrode (acting as the MOF’s metal source) was then placed in a custom-made electrochemical cell compatible with a BioLogic SECM-150 setup (optical images of the electrochemical setup are shown in Figure S2, Supporting Information). The general electrochemical cell design consists of the SECM tip (Pt disc, $10~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ in diameter, enshrouded in a glass shield, $100\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in diameter, $R_{\mathrm{g}}=10^{\circ}$ ) as a second working electrode and $\mathrm{Pt}$ wires as reference and counter electrodes. At first, to determine the tip’s diffusion-limited current, the tip was subject to a cyclic voltammetry (CV) experiment, in a $1\mathrm{ml}$ DMF solution containing $0.2\ \mathrm{~M~}\ \mathrm{LiOCl}_{4}$ as electrolyte and $5\times10^{-3}$ m ferrocene as a fast redox-shuttle (Figure S3, Supporting Information). Thereafter, an approach curve experiment was performed by applying a constant potential over the tip $[0.7\mathrm{~V~}$ vs Pt), while approaching it toward the substrate electrode surface at a speed of $2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . The tip current versus distance data was fitted according to the theoretical model (Figure S4, Supporting Information). By doing so, we were able to determine the substrate-to-tip distance, and thus place the SECM tip at a working distance of ${8\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ above the ${\mathrm{FTO}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ surface. Then, before initializing the localized electrosynthesis process, a $1\mathrm{ml}$ DMF/water (acting as a proton source) $(50/50\mathrm{v\%})$ solution containing $3.16\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~m~}$ of $\mathrm{H_{4}T C P P}$ (MOF ligand) was added to the electrolyte. At this point, the SECM tip was subject to $300~\mathrm{CV}$ cycles at a potential range of $(-0.55\mathrm{~V})-(-1.75\mathrm{~V})$ versus $\mathrm{Pt}_{\mathrm{:}}$ , to reduce $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and produce $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ base equivalents at the vicinity of the ${\mathrm{FTO}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ electrode (Scheme 1, process 1). In turn, the tipproduced base deprotonates the $\mathrm{H_{4}T C P P}$ ligand, as well as reacts with the ${\mathrm{FTO}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ surface to release $\mathbb{A}^{3+}$ ions and initiate the MOF crystallization (Scheme  1 processes 2–3). Consequently, due to the high local concentration of deprotonated ligands and $\mathbb{A}^{3+}$ ions at the substrate–tip gap, the $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ –TCPP MOF will be locally formed at the substrate beneath the SECM tip (Scheme 1, process 4).
The as-formed electro synthesized $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ -TCPP MOFpattern was examined using scanning electron microscopy (SEM). Indeed, as shown in Figure  1a and Figure S5 (Supporting Information), a circle-shaped pattern was formed, having a diameter of ${\approx}100~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , matching the outer SECM tip dimensions (Pt disc and its surrounding glass). Those dimensions can be explained by the higher local concentration of base equivalent in the substrate–tip gap. Furthermore, as can be seen in Figure 1b the as-synthesized MOF possesses the typical flake-like morphology consistent with the previous report of $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ –TCPP grown over alumina surface via a microwaveassisted approach.[18a] These observations suggest the formation of the $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ –TCPP MOF micropattern over the FT-O- ${\bf\cdot A l}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ surface using the SECM.
In addition, energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping analysis of the sample (Figure  2a) clearly shows a uniform high concentration of carbon at the $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ -TCPP pattern, with similar dimensions to the pattern recorded by the SEM images thus pointing for the existence of organic TCPP MOF ligands at the patterned surface. Moreover, as can be seen in Figure  2b, selective area EDS measurements reveal higher carbon concentration inside the deposition pattern compared to outside of the pattern, both in atomic percentage as well as in the Al-to-carbon ratio. Hence, the EDS elemental analysis results hint for the local formation of the $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}.$ –TCPP MOF pattern over the ${\mathrm{FTO}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ electrode surface.
To further characterize the resulting MOF pattern composition, a transmission electron microscopy (TEM)-lamella sample was prepared using a focused ion beam (FIB) technique. As shown in the cross-sectional TEM image in Figure  3a and
Figure S6 (Supporting Information), the lamella is composed of three different layers: i) a bottom FTO substrate, ii) a middle layer composed of the patterned MOF, and iii) a top Pt coating layer originally deposited during the lamella’s FIB preparation process. One can clearly observe the layered structure of the MOF deposit, having a thickness of ${\approx}140{-}170\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Furthermore, electron energy loss spectroscopy (EELS) was performed over the lamella sample, generating an elemental mapping of aluminum, oxygen, and nitrogen. As shown in Figure 3b, there are three main compositional regions: i) the bottom FTO that contains a high amount of oxygen and no aluminum or nitrogen, ii) above the FTO is the $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ -TCPP layer containing aluminum, oxygen, and nitrogen coming from the MOF ligand, and iii) a top Pt layer that essentially does not contain any of the tested elements. An ELLS line scan measurement further provides a depth elemental analysis of the sample (Figure 3c). It can be seen that there is a sharp decrease in oxygen content above the FTO layer followed by a sharp increase in aluminum/ nitrogen content, signaling the $\mathrm{FTO/Al_{2}(O H)_{2}\mathrm{-TCPP}}$ interface (the Pt region contains none of the three elements). This result further supports the successful electrosynthesis of the $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ –TCPP MOF over the FTO surface.
Additional evidence for the successful synthesis of the $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ –TCPP MOF pattern was obtained through Raman spectroscopy and mapping. As shown in Figure  4a, over the deposition pattern $(\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}{-}\mathrm{TCPP})$ , there are two Raman peaks located at $1569~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and $1608~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ that could be assigned to the pyrrole stretching and phenyl group of TCPP MOF ligand $\mathrm{(H_{4}T C P P)}$ , respectively.[32] Those peaks were not detected outside the MOF pattern region $\left(\mathrm{FTO-Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\right)$ , while only a small peak at $630~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ assigned to FTO can be seen.[33] A Raman mapping measurement conducted along with the electrosynthesized MOF pattern, reveals a homogeneous discshaped micropattern (Figure 4b).
Nevertheless, gaining direct proof for the crystallographic structure of the as-formed MOF pattern was challenging due to the small scale of the MOF deposited pattern that ruled out the use of conventional X-ray diffraction (XRD), as well as the physical fragility of the FIB-produced TEM-lamella that did not allow the use of TEM-based electron diffraction measurement. Hence, we turned to a different crystal structure determination method, electron backscatter diffraction (EBSD). In this method, the sample is placed under an electron beam (in an SEM instrument), in which backscatter electrons will form as the beam interacts with the sample. Depending on the specimen’s crystallographic structure, those backscatter electrons will depart from the sample in a specific pattern, as they interact with its crystal lattice, generating the structure-sensitive Kikuchi lines. Indeed, as can be seen in Figure S7 (Supporting Information), EBSD measurements and the Kikuchi lines obtained by the sample correlate well with the $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ –TCPP MOF crystal structure. In other words, this result provides firm evidence for the formation of localized $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ –TCPP MOF at the ${\mathrm{FTO}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ electrode surface. It is important to mention that due to the relatively fast kinetics of MOF formation during electrosynthesis, it is rather challenging to obtain a phase-pure, defect-free crystal structure. Hence, one can expect a MOF deposit containing a certain degree of missing linker/node defect sites.
Next, upon confirming the successful localized electrosynthesis of patterned $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ –TCPP, we turned to gain information regarding its electrocatalytic activity. As a model catalytic reaction, we chose to explore the MOF’s HER performance. To do so, the electrolyte used for $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ –TCPP synthesis was washed from the electrochemical cell and was replaced with a new DMF/water $(80/20,\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}\%)$ solution containing 0.1 m $\mathrm{LiOCl}_{4}$ and $0.1\times10^{-3}$ m HCl (acting as proton source for HER). Then, a substrate generation tip collection (SG/TC) SECM mode was used to detect catalytic hydrogen production on the surface. Generally, a cathodic potential of $-1.18{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (vs NHE) was applied to the MOF-patterned ${\mathrm{FTO}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ electrode to reduce protons and generate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ over the surface. Simultaneously, an anodic potential of $1.32\mathrm{V}$ (vs NHE) was applied to the SECM Pt tip to oxidize the substrate-produced $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (as schematically illustrated in Figure 5a). The SECM tip was then scanned over the surface in the $X–Y$ axis, hovering at ${\approx}10~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ above the surface (to avoid colliding with surface and damage for the tip) while recording the tip current (corresponding to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ generation kinetics), thus producing an HER activity mapping of the MOF-patterned surface (Figure 5b). As can be seen in the HER activity mapping, over the ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}({\mathrm{OH}})_{2}{\mathrm{-TCPP}}$ deposition area, high electrocatalytic HER rates were detected (tip currents of up to $736~\mathrm{pA}$ ). Yet, outside the MOF-patterned region $\left(\mathrm{FTO-Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\right)$ ), significantly lower activity was obtained (tip current ${\approx}180~\mathrm{pA}$ ) due to the alumina coating serving as an insulator over the FTO surface, drastically reducing the surface-generated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production. On the contrary, over the MOF deposition pattern, as the MOF is forming, electrical contact is formed between the underlying FTO support and the MOF layer, resulting in higher surface generated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production and significantly higher tip current, and hence, a high signal-to-noise ratio. Furthermore, we wanted to make sure that the HER signal does not originate from the catalytic activity of exposed FTO pinholes (which may form due to dissolution of the alumina surface and local exposure of the FTO surface). To do so, a control electrosynthesis step was conducted without the $\mathrm{{H_{4}T C P P}}$ linker in the solution. Thereafter, the electrochemical HER performance was mapped in the same manner as described above. As can be seen in Figure S8 (Supporting Information) throughout the entire mapping area, the tip’s current over the sample does not significantly exceed the background current in the $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ –TCPP sample. Therefore, we can conclude that the as-formed $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ -TCPP MOF is indeed responsible for the measured catalytic HER activity.
In addition, the $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ -TCPP electrochemical performance can be further studied using the SECM redox competition (RC) mode.[22] In this mode of operation, both the substrate and tip electrodes simultaneously perform the same electrochemical reaction, and thus compete over the reaction’s reactants. ­Consequently, as the substrate electrochemical activity increases (higher applied overpotential), fewer reactants will be available for the tip, and hence, its catalytic current will drop. Accordingly, we have reasoned that using this mode, the substrate– electrode’s electrocatalytic onset potential could be accurately determined by recording the potential at which the tip’s masstransport limited current starts to decrease. In this manner, one would be able to monitor only the catalytic activity of the substrate while avoiding complications in onset potential determination arising due to non-faradaic, capacitive currents. Thus, we have used the RC mode to measure the HER onset potential of an ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}({\mathrm{OH}})_{2}{\mathrm{-TCPP}}$ micropattern. First, the SECM cell was filled with a DMF/water $(80/20,\mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v}\%)$ solution containing $0.1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{LiOCl}_{4}$ . Then, the tip was placed at $20\upmu\mathrm{m}$ above the substrate’s surface and was subject to a constant cathodic potential of $-1.385\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs NHE) (corresponding to a mass-transport limited HER current at the tip). At the same time, the substrate potential was gradually stepped from $-1.85\mathrm{~V~}$ to $-0.185\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs NHE), to compete with the tip over the $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ reactants, as schematically illustrated in Figure 6a (for more experimental details, see Supporting Information). For each potential applied on the substrate, the change in tip’s current was recorded. As can be seen in Figure 6b, the change in tip’s current starts increasing as the substrate potential increases (higher HER overpotential). To extract the HER onset potential of the $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ –TCPP (substrate electrode), a linear fit of the data was drawn. The fitted line’s intercept with the $x\mathrm{.}$ -axis (applied substrate potential corresponding to no change in tip’s current) determines the MOF’s electrocatalytic HER onset potential $(-0.607\mathrm{V}$ vs NHE).
Finally, in order to prove the generality of our micronscale SECM-based MOF electrosynthesis method, the iconic Cu-based HKUST-1 MOF was also locally synthesized on top of a copper electrode. To achieve that, a cleaned and polished cupper foil electrode was placed in the SECM electrochemical cell described above. As in the case of the $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ –TCPP synthesis procedure, CV and approach curve experiments were performed to extract the tip-to-substrate distance. Thereafter, the SECM tip was placed at $8~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ above the cupper foil surface, and a $1\mathrm{ml}$ of DMF/water $(50/50~\mathrm{v\%})$ solution containing $50\times10^{-3}$ m trimesic-acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ (MOF ligand) was added to the cell. The tip was then subject to $300~\mathrm{CV}$ cycles at a potential range of $(-0.3\mathrm{V})-(-1.5\mathrm{V})$ vs $\mathrm{Pt}$ wire, at $0.1\mathrm{V}\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$ scan rate) to produce $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ base equivalents and deprotonate the MOF ligands in close proximity to the substrate (for more experimental details, see the Experimental Section in Supporting Information). At the same time, base-induced slow dissolution of the copper electrode supplies $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions to the electrolyte solution, thus initializing the formation of HKUST-1 micropattern on the Cu surface (see illustration in Scheme S1, Supporting Information). Indeed, as can be seen in the SEM images in Figure S8A,B (Supporting Information), local deposition of HKUST-1 with a pattern diameter of ${\approx}87~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ was generated using this method, practically matching the SECM tip’s outer diameter. Moreover, a higher magnification SEM image (Figure S8C, Supporting Information) shows that the deposited material exhibits a typical HKUST-1 octahedron crystal morphology.[34] EDS mapping conducted over the deposited pattern illustrates higher carbon content at the deposition area, thus further indicating the MOF formation (Figure S9, Supporting Information). In addition, EBSD maseurmnts over the HKUST-1 deposition pattern also indicate succesful MOF formation, as can be seen by the obtained Kikuchi lines that correlate well with the HKUST-1 MOF crystal structure (Figure S10, Supporting Information).
As such, the obtained results point to a successful HKUST-1 micropatterning deposition and show the generality and flexibility of the SECM-induced MOF micropatterning technique.
## 3. Conclusion
In conclusion, here we have developed a new, general method for spatially confined electrosynthesis and subsequent electrochemical characterization of MOF micropatterns using SECM. Two different MOF structures, $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ -TCPP and HKUST-1, were locally electro-synthesized on alumina and cupper electrodes respectively. The resulting MOF patterns chemical composition and crystal structure were characterized using SEM, EDS, EBSD, TEM, and Raman spectroscopy. Moreover, taking advantage of the unique capabilities of SECM, we were able to investigate the electrocatalytic activity of the as-synthesized $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ –TCPP MOF. First, by electrochemically mapping its performance toward HER using the SG/TC mode with an excellent signalto-noise ratio. Second, via accurate determination of the MOF’s HER onset potential using the RC measurement mode. The ability to combine the patterned fabrication with in situ electrochemical characterization will accelerate MOF’s research toward energy conversion-related applications and will expend our knowledge on the fundamental mechanisms governing MOFbased electrocatalytic reactions. In addition, The presented localized electrosynthesis method could also be found useful for other types of applications, as it will add new means for fabrication of patterned MOF thin films and can pave the way toward their utilization in a variety of microelectronic devices, sensor arrays, as well as in tandem-based electrocatalytic schemes.
